📗
  📐 The arithmetic that turns the digits of a number into a real, and a real
  into digits, each time to the nearest there is.

  One multiplication or division of two reals gives the nearest real to the
  true result. A chain of them does not: each step rounds, and the roundings
  add up, which is how 0.07 came to be read as the real next to it. So a
  number that needs more than one step is kept here in two reals, the second
  holding what the first had to round away. That is good to about thirty
  digits, and only the last step rounds.

  Two things about reals in this compiler shape the code. Every comparison
  with something that is not a number is true, so a loop must never wait for
  one to turn false. And there are no flags to relax arithmetic, so what is
  written here is computed exactly as written.
